      "problem": "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(4,3),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,5),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,4)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(4,3),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(8,5),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,4),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-8-1 \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 5+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+5+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3} & ∼N(-5,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
